aregivers can find and download NCCN Guidelines for Patients across cancer types and supportive-care topics. Its description emphasizes broad coverage (screening, diagnosis, treatment planning, side-effect management, survivorship-related considerations) and repeated use as the canonical destination for “more information.” [Data: Entities (64, 1109); Relationships (5696, 1767, 6084, 11459, 5276, +more)].\\n\\nMultiple guideline documents and topic-specific resources are explicitly linked to NCCN.org/patientguidelines as their access point, including adolescent and young adult cancer guidance, bone cancer (2025), soft tissue sarcoma, distress during cancer care, bladder cancer, and others—indicating that the portal is the primary distribution and discovery mechanism in the network rather than a single-topic webpage. [Data: Entities (1838, 5184, 2859, 4241, 2858); Relationships (5696, 11459, 5276, 8870, 5274, +more)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App is a parallel distribution channel tightly coupled to the web portal\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App is described as a centralized mobile access platform for NCCN patient-oriented educational materials across many cancers and supportive-care topics. This app is repeatedly referenced as a place patients and caregivers can access guideline-based guidance, mirroring the portal’s role while improving accessibility through mobile delivery. [Data: Entities (35)].\\n\\nThe app’s close coupling to NCCN.org/patientguidelines is directly represented by an explicit relationship stating that both are referenced as sources where additional information can be found. Specific patient guideline resources (e.g., colorectal cancer screening and immunotherapy side effects) are also directly connected to the app as an access pathway, reinforcing that the community’s “technical capability” is largely informational distribution across web and mobile formats. [Data: Entities (35, 64, 2675, 4925); Relationships (1510, 4932, 10876)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Fertility preservation is a major thematic cluster, with targeted emphasis on adolescents and young adults\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Fertility preservation appears as a defined set of actions and strategies intended to keep future reproductive options open when cancer treatment may affect fertility (e.g., sperm banking, egg/embryo freezing, ovarian tissue banking, ovarian transposition). The community ties this topic to NCCN’s patient-guideline infrastructure, explicitly directing readers to NCCN.org/patientguidelines for additional patient-focused fertility preservation details. [Data: Entities (1495, 64); Relationships (2429)].\\n\\nThe fertility preservation cluster includes multiple support roles and populations: fertility and reproductive specialists (who guide patients through option evaluation and planning), fertility counselors (who discuss options before treatment), and explicit references to adults plus adolescents/young adults as target audiences. This highlights a “care-navigation” dimension within the community—connecting educational materials to the types of professionals involved in decision support. [Data: Entities (1488, 5695, 1530, 3727, 5707); Relationships (2413, 12773, 2431, 7660, 12778, +more)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The community spans both disease-specific guidance and supportive-care/quality-of-life guidance\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Beyond disease-specific patient guidelines, the ecosystem explicitly includes supportive-care and quality-of-life topics. Examples represented here include distress during cancer care and immunotherapy side effects (including immune checkpoint inhibitors such as PD-1 inhibitors), showing that the network is intended to help patients understand not only cancer diagnoses but also treatment experience and adverse effects. [Data: Entities (4241, 4925, 64); Relationships (8870, 928)].\\n\